<Project>

  <!-- Why the same .sql file has to be BOTH an EmbeddedResource and an AdditionalFile.

       They are two different consumers reading the same bytes at two different times, and neither
       channel can serve the other:

         * EmbeddedResource is the RUN-TIME channel. The procedure body has to travel inside the
           assembly, because RegisterStoredProcedures(assembly, prefix) and EmbeddedScriptApplier read
           it back through Assembly.GetManifestResourceStream at startup. That is the shipping copy.

         * AdditionalFiles is the COMPILE-TIME channel. The source generator needs the same text to
           read each parameter's name, type, length, precision, scale and OUTPUT direction out of the
           procedure header, so the developer does not have to spell them out a second time as [Sql]
           attributes. A generator cannot open the embedded resource - at generation time the assembly
           does not exist yet - and it cannot open the file off disk either, because the generator
           project sets EnforceExtendedAnalyzerRules and RS1035 makes System.IO.File a compile ERROR.
           AdditionalFiles is the only channel Roslyn gives an analyzer for file content.

       So the file is listed twice, and that is correct rather than redundant: one entry ships the
       bytes, the other shows them to the compiler. Nothing is duplicated in the output - the
       /additionalfile: entry produces no assembly content at all.

       Derived FROM EmbeddedResource and never the reverse: adding a second EmbeddedResource for the
       same file is NETSDK1022/CS1508. This is additive - the /resource: entry and its logical name are
       untouched, so RegisterStoredProcedures(assembly, prefix) and EmbeddedScriptApplier keep reading
       the same one file at run time.

       A bare ItemGroup, not a Target: AdditionalFiles has to exist at evaluation time or the IDE
       project system does not see it without a design-time build. AdditionalFileItemNames is the
       other route and is worse - it promotes an entire item type wholesale, and Roslyn's own targets
       flag it as a workaround (dotnet/roslyn#12223).

       Set R21SqlScriptDiscovery=false to switch the whole feature off and go back to spelling every
       [Sql] out, which keeps working forever. -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(R21SqlScriptDiscovery)' != 'false'">
    <AdditionalFiles Include="@(EmbeddedResource->WithMetadataValue('Extension','.sql'))"
                     Exclude="@(AdditionalFiles)" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
